the fermentation of dietary fibers, contributing to the digestive processes in various organisms. Additionally, Ruminococcus has been associated with decreased amounts in obese individuals, suggesting a potential link between its presence and body weight regulation.BIFIDOBACTERIUM:Bifidobacterium is a genus of bacteria that plays a crucial role in promoting gut health. It is commonly found in the intestines and is known for its beneficial effects, including the modulation of inflammation and metabolic processes. Research has shown that Bifidobacterium is negatively correlated with obesity and diabetes, with lower quantities of this bacterium observed in obese individuals. This genus is frequently utilized in probiotic treatments, highlighting its importance in maintaining a healthy gut microbiome. Bifidobacterium belongs to the phylum Actinobacteria and is recognized for its contributions to overall gut health, making it a significant focus of studies related to obesity and metabolic health.PROTEOBACTERIA:Proteobacteria is a major phylum of bacteria that encompasses a diverse range of pathogens and plays a significant role in the context of gut microbiota. Research studies have indicated that Proteobacteria can exhibit changes in response to antibiotic administration, highlighting its dynamic nature and importance in microbial ecology and health.FILO: FIRMICUTES:NI√ëOS AFRICANOS:Grupo de ni√±os que presentan una microbiota intestinal rica en Bacteroidetes y con una mayor riqueza microbiana en comparaci√≥n con los ni√±os europeos, debido a su dieta alta en fibra y baja en grasas animales.NI√ëOS EUROPEOS:Grupo de ni√±os que presentan una microbiota intestinal caracterizada por una mayor proporci√≥n de Firmicutes y una menor riqueza microbiana, atribuida a su dieta alta en grasas y prote√≠nas de origen animal y baja en fibra.DIETA ALTA EN FIBRA:Patr√≥n diet√©tico que se asocia con un aumento en la proporci√≥n de Bacteroidetes y una disminuci√≥n en Firmicutes en la microbiota intestinal.ALTERACIONES DE LA ENERG√çA:Bloque que describe c√≥mo los cambios en la dieta afectan la microbiota intestinal y la energ√≠a del cuerpo.PATRONES DIET√âTICOS:Factores que influyen en la composici√≥n de la microbiota intestinal, mostrando diferencias significativas entre grupos con h√°bitos diet√©ticos diferentes.MICROBIOTA DE COMUNIDADES OCCIDENTALES:Descripci√≥n de la microbiota intestinal de comunidades occidentales, caracterizada por una ingesta alta en grasa y prote√≠nas de origen animal y un bajo contenido en fibra.MICROBIOTA DE COMUNIDADES ORIENTALES:Descripci√≥n de la microbiota intestinal de comunidades orientales, que muestra una mayor riqueza microbiana y una mayor proporci√≥n de Bacteroidetes.DIETA ALTA EN GRASAS:BACTERIANA:Bacteriana is a research entity focused on the study of intestinal microbiota and its relation to obesity and metabolic diseases.AKKERMANSIA MUCINIPHILA:Akkermansia muciniphila is a beneficial bacterium that plays a significant role in gut health and metabolic processes. This species of bacteria is known for its ability to degrade mucin, a component of the mucus layer in the gut, and is linked to improved metabolic profiles, particularly in relation to obesity and diabetes. Research indicates that higher levels of Akkermansia muciniphila correlate with better metabolic health outcomes in rodents, especially when its abundance is normalized through dietary changes and prebiotic intake.

Interestingly, Akkermansia muciniphila can also increase in abundance following broad-spectrum antibiotic treatment, which may have implications for reducing the incidence of diabetes. Overall, this bacterium is associated with the gut microbiota and is considered important for metabolic health, with potential applications in obesity prevention and treatment.ENDOTOXEMIA METAB√ìLICA:Endotoxemia metab√≥lica refers to the presence of endotoxins in the bloodstream, often linked to inflammation and metabolic disorders.S√çNTESIS DE √ÅCIDOS GRASOS DE CADENA CORTA:Synthesis of short-chain fatty acids (SCFA) is a metabolic process that occurs in the gut, associated with various health benefits including improved gut health and reduced inflammation.MICROBIOTA:Microbiota refers to the community of microorganisms, including bacteria, that inhabit various environments within the human body, with a significant concentration residing in the gut. This community plays a vital role in several aspects of health, including digestion, metabolism, and the development of allergic diseases. The composition of the microbiota can be influenced by various factors, such as antibiotic use, which can alter its balance. Additionally, the microbiota is notably affected in conditions like obesity, where it can impact metabolic functions and contribute to the overall health of an individual.NEYRINCK:Neyrinck is a researcher who studied the effects of broad-spectrum antibiotics on obese rodents and their metabolic alterations.DEKZENNE:Dekzenne is a researcher who collaborated with Neyrinck in studying the effects of antibiotics on the microbiota of obese rodents.CANI:Cani is a researcher who supports the idea that broad-spectrum antibiotic therapy significantly reduces plasma levels of lipopolysaccharides in animal models.MEMBREZ:Membrez is a researcher who studied the effects of antibiotic treatment on genetically obese rodents with insulin resistance.H. PYLORI:H. pylori, or Helicobacter pylori, is a bacterium that can infect humans, particularly the stomach. It is often associated with various gastrointestinal diseases, including ulcers, and has implications for iron absorption. Additionally, H. pylori has been mentioned in the context of antibiotic exposure, which may influence weight gain. This bacterium plays a significant role in gastrointestinal health and disease, highlighting its importance in medical research and treatment.ANTIBIOTIC THERAPY:Antibiotic therapy is a treatment method employed to combat bacterial infections, frequently initiated empirically in severe cases. This approach involves the use of antibiotics to address infections and has been the subject of research regarding its effects on microbiota and potential weight gain.INFECCIONES BACTERIANAS:Infecciones bacterianas refer to bacterial infections that antibiotics are used to treat, which can impact microbiota diversity.ALERGIAS:Alergias refer to allergies, which are immune system reactions to specific substances. These reactions can be influenced by various factors, including genetic predispositions and environmental conditions. Additionally, changes in microbiota due to antibiotic use may also play a role in the development of allergies. Among the different types of allergies, food allergies are notable as they represent adverse immune responses to certain foods. These food allergies can manifest in early childhood and may vary in duration and severity over time.DIVERSIDAD FILOGEN√âTICA:Diversidad filogen√©tica refers to the genetic diversity of microbial communities, which can be altered by antibiotic treatment.SISTEMA INMUNE:El sistema inmune es el conjunto de c√©lulas y mecanismos que protegen al organismo contra infecciones y enfermedades, incluyendo reacciones al√©rgicas. Este sistema es fundamental para la defensa del cuerpo y su desarrollo en ni√±os puede influir en la prevalencia de alergias. Adem√°s, el sistema inmune es una red compleja de c√©lulas y prote√≠nas que defiende al organismo contra infecciones, y su desarrollo y mantenimiento est√°n influenciados por la microbiota intestinal. Cambios en esta microbiota, como los provocados por el uso de antibi√≥ticos, pueden afectar el funcionamiento del sistema inmune.ROEDORES:GENES CANDIDATOS:Los genes candidatos son aquellos identificados en estudios de asociaci√≥n que ayudan a entender la sensibilidad y resistencia a la diabetes y obesidad.DIVERSIDAD GEN√âTICA:La diversidad gen√©tica se refiere a las variaciones en los genes entre individuos, lo que puede influir en la susceptibilidad a enfermedades metab√≥licas.FACTORES AMBIENTALES:Los factores ambientales son condiciones externas que pueden afectar la salud, incluyendo la dieta y el estilo de vida, y est√°n relacionados con el aumento de la obesidad.BUTIRATO:Butirato, also known as butyrate, is a short-chain fatty acid produced by gut bacteria during the fermentation of dietary fiber. It plays a crucial role in energy metabolism by providing energy to colon epithelial cells and reducing intestinal permeability. Additionally, butirato is involved in the apoptosis of inflammatory cells and enhances the function of the mucosal barrier, contributing to inflammation reduction and overall gut health.LIPOPOLISAC√ÅRIDOS:Lipopolysaccharides (LPS) are pro-inflammatory components found in the cell wall of gram-negative bacteria. They are associated with increased intestinal permeability and are linked to chronic inflammation, which is often related to obesity.GHRLINA:Ghrelin is a hormone that stimulates appetite, its levels are influenced by gut microbiota composition and dietary intake.BIFIDOBACTERIAS:Bifidobacteria are beneficial gut bacteria that play a crucial role in digestion and maintaining gut health. They are associated with lower levels of ghrelin, a hormone that regulates appetite. These bacteria are typically found in the intestines and their populations can be increased by diets rich in prebiotics, which support their growth and activity. Overall, Bifidobacteria contribute positively to digestive health and metabolic regulation.LACTOBACILUS:Lactobacillus is a genus of bacteria that is part of the gut microbiota, known for its role in fermenting carbohydrates and producing beneficial metabolites.BLOQUE: ALTERACIONES DE LA ENERG√çA:This section discusses the alterations in energy balance related to dietary interventions and their effects on host metabolism.NFKB:NFKB, also known as nuclear factor kappa-light-chain-enhancer of activated B cells, is a protein complex that plays a crucial role in regulating immune response and inflammation. It is essential for controlling the transcription of DNA, thereby influencing the immune response to infections. Through its regulatory functions, NFKB is integral to the body's ability to respond to various immune challenges.TLR4:Toll-like receptor 4 (TLR4) is a protein that plays a crucial role in the immune system by recognizing pathogens and activating inflammatory responses.MACR√ìFAGO:Macrophages are immune cells that engulf and digest cellular debris and pathogens, playing a vital role in the immune response and inflammation.FIAF:FIAF, or fat-induced adipocyte factor, is a gene expressed in the intestine that plays a crucial role in regulating fat metabolism. It inhibits lipoprotein lipase activity, which in turn affects fat storage in both the liver and adipose tissue. Additionally, FIAF is a protein that is influenced by the gut microbiota, highlighting its connection to the body's metabolic processes and the impact of intestinal health on fat regulation.ACIDOS BILIARES:Bile acids are produced in the liver and play a role in fat digestion and metabolism, with primary and secondary forms affecting energy expenditure.RECEPTOR TOLL-LIKE 4:TLR4 is a receptor that recognizes lipopolysaccharides from gram-negative bacteria, activating inflammatory pathways.PROBIOTICOS:Probiotics are beneficial bacteria that can modulate gut microbiota composition and enhance butyrate production.GLP-2:GLP-2, or glucagon-like peptide-2, is a hormone peptide that plays a crucial role in promoting intestinal growth and protecting against intestinal atrophy. This peptide hormone is integral to the regulation of gut function and energy homeostasis, working in conjunction with GLP-1 to support these processes. Overall, GLP-2 is essential for maintaining intestinal health and functionality.PYY:PYY (peptide YY) is an intestinal hormone that helps regulate appetite and energy balance.YADAV ET AL:Yadav et al are researchers who demonstrated the modulation of intestinal microbiota composition through probiotics, stimulating butyrate production and improving inflammatory status and insulin resistance.PEPTIDO INTESTINAL YY:Peptido intestinal YY is a hormone involved in reducing appetite and regulating energy balance.SCFA:Short-chain fatty acids (SCFA) are fatty acids characterized by having fewer than six carbon atoms. They are produced during the fermentation of dietary fibers in the gut by gut bacteria. SCFAs play a crucial role in maintaining gut health, contributing to various physiological processes and overall well-being.GPR43:GPR43 is a G protein-coupled receptor that mediates the effects of SCFA on glucose metabolism and appetite regulation.GPR41:GPR41 is a G protein-coupled receptor that is involved in the signaling pathways activated by SCFA.GORDON ET AL:Gordon et al are researchers who studied the effects of microbial colonization on body weight and fat in germ-free mice.B√ÑCKHED ET AL:B√§ckhed et al are researchers who contributed to the understanding of the relationship between gut microbiota and obesity.ACTINOBACTERIA:Actinobacteria is a phylum of bacteria that has been linked to obesity, showing increased representation in obese individuals.POLISAC√ÅRIDOS INDIGERIBLES:Indigestible polysaccharides are substances that require microbiota to convert them into absorbable monosaccharides and short-chain fatty acids (SCFA), playing a role in energy extraction.M. SMITHII:M. smithii is a type of Archaea that converts hydrogen into methane, playing a role in the digestion of indigestible polysaccharides.RUMINCOCCUS:TURNBAUGH PJ:Turnbaugh PJ is a prominent researcher recognized for his significant contributions to the understanding of the human microbiome and its impact on health. He has conducted important experiments that demonstrate the causal relationship between microbiota and obesity, highlighting how microbial ecology is associated with this condition. Through his work, Turnbaugh PJ has advanced the field of microbiome research, particularly in relation to its implications for obesity and overall health.AKKERMANSIA:Akkermansia is a genus of bacteria that has been associated with gut health and obesity.RATONES:Experiments involving mice were conducted to study the effects of microbiota on obesity.GEMELOS HOMOCIG√ìTICOS DISCORDANTES:Gemelos homocig√≥ticos discordantes son gemelos que tienen diferentes caracter√≠sticas de obesidad, utilizados en estudios sobre microbiota y obesidad.RATONES GEN√âTICAMENTE ID√âNTICOS:Rats that are genetically identical used in experiments to study the effects of diet on microbiota and obesity.LACTOBACILLUS RHAMNOSUS:Lactobacillus rhamnosus is a specific strain of Lactobacillus that has shown beneficial effects on obesity.LACTOBACILLUS GASSERI:Lactobacillus gasseri is another strain of Lactobacillus that has demonstrated positive effects on obesity and metabolic health.ACINETOBACTER:Acinetobacter is a genus of bacteria known for its role in causing infections, particularly in healthcare settings, where it poses a significant risk to patients with invasive devices. In addition to its pathogenic potential, Acinetobacter has been positively associated with obesity and has been the subject of research concerning metabolic health and obesity. This dual role highlights the importance of understanding Acinetobacter not only as a potential infectious agent but also in the context of metabolic disorders.ALIIVIBRIO:Aliivibrio is a genus of bacteria that has been positively associated with obesity and may have implications for gut health. This connection suggests that Aliivibrio could play a significant role in understanding the relationship between gut microbiota and obesity, highlighting its potential importance in studies related to metabolic health and dietary impacts.MARINOMONAS:Marinomonas is a genus of bacteria that has been positively associated with obesity and has been studied for its potential effects on health. This genus is of interest in the field of microbiology due to its implications in understanding the relationship between gut microbiota and metabolic conditions.PSEUDOALTEROMONAS:Pseudoalteromonas is a genus of bacteria that has garnered attention for its potential implications in health and disease. Notably, it has been positively associated with obesity, suggesting a significant role in metabolic processes. Research surrounding Pseudoalteromonas continues to explore its various effects on human health, highlighting its importance in understanding the complex interactions between gut microbiota and overall well-being.KALLIOM√ÑKI ET AL:Kalliom√§ki et al conducted a study showing a higher proportion of Bifidobacterium and lower Staphylococcus aureus in normal-weight children compared to those who developed overweight over the years.GAMMAPROTEOBACTERIA:Gammaproteobacteria is a class of bacteria that showed significant increase in subjects after Roux-en-Y gastric bypass surgery.MICROBIOME:Microbiome refers to the collective genomes of the microbiota, influencing energy absorption and nutrient deposition in the host.SHEWANELLA:Shewanella is a genus of bacteria that has been positively associated with obesity.LACHNOSPIRA:Lachnospira is a genus of bacteria that has been positively associated with obesity.CITOBACTER:Citrobacter is a genus of bacteria that has been positively associated with obesity.SHIGELLA:Shigella is a genus of bacteria known for causing bacillary dysentery, which is characterized by diarrhea that contains blood and mucus. Additionally, Shigella has been positively associated with obesity, indicating a potential link between this bacterial genus and weight-related health issues.PARABACTEROIDES DISTASONIS:Parabacteroides distasonis is a species of bacteria that has been positively associated with obesity.E. COLI:E. coli, or Escherichia coli, is a species of bacteria that has a complex relationship with human health. It has been positively associated with obesity, indicating that certain strains may play a role in weight regulation. Additionally, some strains of E. coli can be utilized as probiotics, contributing to gut health by promoting a balanced microbiome. This dual nature highlights the importance of distinguishing between different strains of E. coli, as their effects on health can vary significantly.PREVOTELLA:Prevotella is a genus of bacteria that has been negatively associated with obesity.SUTTERELLA:Sutterella is a genus of bacteria that has been negatively associated with obesity.METHANOBREVIPA:Methanobrevibacter is a genus of bacteria that has been negatively associated with obesity.LACTOBACILLUS REUTERI:Lactobacillus reuteri is a type of beneficial bacteria that is found in the gut microbiota. It has been associated with various studies related to obesity, highlighting its potential role in influencing metabolic health and weight management.HUMAN GUT:The human gut is the digestive tract where the microbiota resides and plays a significant role in nutrient absorption and metabolism.STOMBAUGH JI:Stombaugh JI is a co-author of a study on the diversity of the human intestinal microbial flora published in Science.GORDON JI:Gordon JI is a researcher dedicated to exploring the role of gut microbes in human health and obesity. He has made significant contributions to the understanding of host-bacterial mutualism within the human intestine, highlighting the intricate relationships between humans and their gut microbiota. Through his work, Gordon JI aims to shed light on how these microbial communities influence various aspects of health, particularly in relation to obesity.JANSSON JK:Jansson JK is a co-author of research on the human gut microbiota.KNIGHT R:Knight R is a researcher recognized for his significant contributions to the study of microbial communities and their diversity. He has a particular focus on the human gut microbiome, exploring its implications for health and disease. Through his work, Knight R has advanced the understanding of how these microbial populations interact and influence various biological processes.ECKBURG PB:Eckburg PB is a co-author of a study on the diversity of the human intestinal microbial flora.B√ÑCKHED F:B√§ckhed F is a researcher known for his studies on host-bacterial mutualism in the human intestine. He has also investigated the mechanisms of resistance to diet-induced obesity in germ-free mice, contributing to the understanding of how gut bacteria interact with the host and influence metabolic processes.GUARNER F:Guarner F is a researcher who has written about the role of intestinal flora in health and disease.COSTELLO EK:Costello EK is a researcher who studied bacterial community variation in human body habitats.PETERSON J:Peterson J is a co-author of the NIH Human Microbiome Project study.LOZUPONE CA:NIH HUMAN MICROBIOME PROJECT:The NIH Human Microbiome Project is a research initiative aimed at understanding the human microbiome and its role in health and disease.META HIT CONSORTIUM:The MetaHIT Consortium is a collaborative research project focused on the human gut microbiome and its genetic diversity.TURNBAUGH, P.J.:P.J. Turnbaugh is a researcher involved in multiple studies related to the human microbiome and its implications for health.HAMADY, M.:M. Hamady is a researcher who has contributed to studies on the human microbiome, particularly in relation to gut health.QIN, J.:J. Qin is a researcher known for his work on the human gut microbial gene catalogue established by metagenomics sequencing.BENSON, A.K.:A.K. Benson is a researcher who has studied the individuality of gut microbiota composition and its genetic influences.KOENIG, J.E.:J.E. Koenig is a researcher focused on the succession of microbial consortia in the developing infant gut microbiome.BURKE, C.:C. Burke is a researcher who has studied bacterial community assembly based on functional genes.ADLERBERTH, I.:I. Adlerberth is a researcher who has investigated the establishment of gut microbiota in Western infants.DOMINGUEZ-BELLO, M.G.:M.G. Dominguez-Bello is a researcher known for her work on how delivery mode shapes the acquisition of microbiota in newborns.HUUURE, A.:A. Huurre is a researcher focused on the study of gut microbiota and its implications for health. Their research includes investigating the effects of different modes of delivery on gut microbiota and immunity, contributing to a deeper understanding of how these factors influence overall health outcomes.GUT MICROBIOME:The gut microbiome refers to the complex community of microorganisms residing in the gastrointestinal tract, which plays a crucial role in human health.NATURE:Nature is a prominent scientific journal recognized for its high-quality research publications across a wide array of fields, including biology, medicine, and environmental sciences. The journal features studies on significant topics such as the gut microbiome and obesity, as well as research focused on the human microbiome. Through its diverse range of articles, Nature contributes to the advancement of scientific knowledge and understanding in various disciplines.PROCEEDINGS OF THE NATIONAL ACADEMY OF SCIENCES:Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences (PNAS) is a scientific journal that publishes research articles, including those related to the human microbiome.ACTA PAEDIATRICA:Acta Paediatrica is a journal that publishes research related to pediatrics, including studies on the establishment of gut microbiota in infants.PLOS BIOLOGY:PLOS Biology is an open-access journal that publishes research articles across all areas of biology, including studies on the development of the human infant intestinal microbiota.APPLIED AND ENVIRONMENTAL MICROBIOLOGY:Applied and Environmental Microbiology is a journal that publishes research on microbiology, including studies on the transmission of gut bacteria from mother to infant.TRENDS IN MICROBIOLOGY:Trends in Microbiology is a journal that publishes reviews and research articles on microbiology, including the development of intestinal microbiota in infants.GENOME BIOLOGY AND EVOLUTION:Genome Biology and Evolution is a journal dedicated to publishing research in the fields of evolutionary biology and genomics. It encompasses a wide range of studies, including those focused on gut microbiota and microbiomes. The journal serves as a platform for disseminating significant findings that contribute to the understanding of evolutionary processes and the genetic underpinnings of various biological phenomena.KALLIOMAKI, M.:M. Kalliomaki is a researcher who has contributed to studies on the gut microbiota and its implications for health.RAUTAVA, S.:S. Rautava is a researcher focused on the impact of delivery mode on gut microbiota and immunity.RINNE, M.:M. Rinne is a researcher who has studied the effects of delivery mode on gut microbiota.SALMINEN:Salminen is a researcher involved in studies on gut microbiota and health outcomes.RAVEL, J.:J. Ravel is a researcher known for work on the vaginal microbiome of reproductive-age women.CLAESSON, M.J.:M.J. Claesson is a researcher who has studied the intestinal microbiota of the elderly.DE FILIPPO, C.:C. De Filippo is a researcher who has investigated the impact of diet on gut microbiota in children.HARAKEH, S.M.:S.M. Harakeh is a researcher studying the relationship between gut microbiota and obesity.SHEN, W.:W. Shen is a researcher fo